# Pairs Trading: Statistical Arbitrage

A research pipeline in Python for pairs trading. It finds same-industry stock pairs with
cointegration tests and controls for multiple testing. It estimates hedge ratios by
static OLS, rolling OLS and a hand-written Kalman filter, and fits Ornstein–Uhlenbeck
half-lives. Both legs are traded through the event-driven backtester from project 02,
with rolling formation and trading cohorts. Results are reported after costs, out of
sample, with checks on neutrality and pair stability.

> **This is educational research software. It is not production-ready.** It does not
> connect to a broker and places no trades. Statistical arbitrage is **not** risk-free
> arbitrage: pairs stop being cointegrated, spreads widen further, and costs, borrow and
> financing can turn a stationary spread into a loss. The synthetic results show that
> the software works as designed. **They are not evidence of market alpha.**
> **Market-data result: no out-of-sample edge after costs** (see below).

![single pair](results/figures/syn_04_single_pair_price_spread_z.png)

## Status: implemented, tested, executed

| Area | Status |
|---|---|
| Synthetic data A–E (cointegrated, independent RWs, correlated RWs, breakdown, time-varying β) and a sector universe with known truth | Implemented, tested, executed |
| Universe filters (history, gaps, recency, liquidity, currency, optional point-in-time membership) with rejection reasons | Implemented, tested, executed |
| Engle–Granger (MacKinnon p-values, predeclared orientation, optional Bonferroni over both directions), ADF diagnostics, Johansen (rank 0/1/2, normalised vector) | Implemented, tested, executed |
| BH and BY FDR per formation date; raw / adjusted / filtered / selected stages kept separate | Implemented, tested, executed; FDP measured against synthetic truth |
| OU fit (validity statuses, half-life CI), Hurst, Lo–MacKinlay variance ratio | Implemented, tested, executed, null-calibrated |
| Static OLS, causal rolling OLS, NumPy Kalman (Joseph form), pykalman cross-check | Implemented, tested (pykalman agrees to 1e-8 in tests, ~1e-13 in the example) |
| Causal z-scores, 6-state signal machine (entry/exit/stop/time stop/breakdown/cool-down/missing data/expiry) | Implemented, tested |
| β → holdings (beta-weighted or dollar-neutral), rounding audit, admission limits (gross, net, name, sector, pairs per name) | Implemented, tested |
| Project-02 backtester integration (thin adapter, next-open fills, two-leg confirmation, legging unwind, netting across pairs) | Implemented, tested, executed |
| Rolling cohorts in one shared account; pair-level P&L reconciled to the account (residual < 1e-8) | Implemented, tested, executed |
| Metrics, gross vs net, cost sensitivity, threshold grid (validation-tuned + exploratory), neutrality (HAC), stability, cohort audit | Implemented, executed |
| Optional yfinance market mode | Implemented, **executed** (32 US stocks, 2012–2025) |
| Locked hold-out with confirm-only evaluation | Implemented, tested; **never evaluated** |
| Research mode (64 synthetic stocks, monthly cohorts) | Implemented, **not executed** |
| Distance method, PCA residuals, copulas, regime filters, optimal OU thresholds, intraday | **Not implemented** |

## Installation (Windows PowerShell; macOS/Linux: `source .venv/bin/activate`)

Python 3.11 or later. The project-02 backtester must sit next to this folder
(`../backtester`). It is installed from a wheel built in a temporary copy: nothing is
written into the backtester's folder and Git is not used.

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
```
```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```
```powershell
python scripts/install_local_backtester.py
```
```powershell
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,kalman-ref,market]"
```

(`--path D:\elsewhere\backtester` points the installer at another location.)

## Commands

| What | Command |
|---|---|
| Fast offline demo (everything, synthetic, ~5–10 min) | `python scripts/run_all.py` |
| Generate synthetic data only | `python scripts/generate_synthetic_data.py` |
| Screening + stability | `python scripts/run_screening.py` |
| Single pair | `python scripts/run_single_pair.py` |
| Portfolio | `python scripts/run_portfolio.py` |
| Hedge-method comparison | `python scripts/run_hedge_comparison.py` |
| Costs + threshold grid | `python scripts/run_sensitivity.py` |
| Market data (optional) | `python scripts/download_market_data.py` then `python scripts/run_all.py --data market` |
| Larger research mode | `python scripts/run_all.py --mode research` |
| Rebuild + execute notebooks | `python scripts/build_notebooks.py` |
| Tests (all / fast only) | `python -m pytest` / `python -m pytest -m "not slow"` |
| Lint, types | `python -m ruff check src tests scripts` / `python -m mypy` |
| CLI equivalent | `pairs run-all --data synthetic`, `pairs screen`, `pairs portfolio`, … |
| Hold-out (deliberately separate) | `python scripts/run_holdout.py --confirm` |

## Results

Every figure carries a stamp at the bottom with data mode, universe, dates, cohort
design, hedge method, execution convention, costs and sample label. The numbers behind
each figure are in `results/tables/figure_data/`. Versions, configs, seed, provenance and
runtimes are in `results/run_metadata.json`.

**Common settings.** 12-month formation and 6-month trading windows with a new cohort
every quarter (two overlapping cohorts, each limited to half of a 1.0× gross budget).
Signal at the close, fill at the next open. Net costs per leg: 1 bp commission, half of
a 4 bp spread, 1 bp slippage; borrow 0.5%/yr; cash 2%, debit 4%. Sharpe is computed on
excess returns over the 2% cash rate (gross: rf = 0).

### Synthetic (24 stocks, 4 sectors, one pure-null sector). OOS test 2017–2021, a-priori thresholds

| | Gross | Net |
|---|---|---|
| CAGR (net includes ~2% cash interest) | 3.8% | 5.4% |
| Sharpe | 2.09 | 1.87 |
| Max drawdown | 1.4% | 1.0% |
| Avg gross exposure / time invested | 0.12 / 57% | 0.12 / 57% |
| Completed pair trades / avg holding | 158 / 9.2 sessions | 158 / 9.2 sessions |
| Trading costs / borrow | 0 | 16.8k / 1.9k (on 1M) |
| Market beta (HAC 95% CI) | | −0.006 [−0.013, 0.001] |

![equity](results/figures/syn_08_equity_gross_net.png)
![tearsheet](results/figures/syn_10_tearsheet.png)

### Market (32 US large caps, 7 industries, yfinance adjusted). OOS test 2018–2023, a-priori thresholds

| | Gross | Net |
|---|---|---|
| CAGR | −0.2% | 1.7% (below the 2% cash rate) |
| Sharpe | −0.31 | **−0.47** |
| Max drawdown | 2.1% | 1.3% |
| Completed pair trades / hit rate | 32 / 50% | 32 / 50% |
| Avg gross exposure | 0.03 | 0.03 |
| Market beta (HAC 95% CI) | | +0.003 [−0.001, 0.006] |

![market equity](results/figures/mkt_08_equity_gross_net.png)

### Screening funnel and FDR

| | candidates | raw EG p<0.05 | BH q=0.10 | + filters | selected | dates with no pair |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Synthetic (34 dates) | 1,845 | 454 | 199 | 190 | 96 | 7 |
| Market (42 dates) | 2,688 | 232 (8.6%) | 39 | 38 | 27 | 24 |

With known synthetic truth, the mean false-discovery proportion was **0.50 raw, 0.29
BH, 0.10 BY**. BY power was only 3.8%, against 22% for BH. In other words, BH at q = 0.10
did **not** control FDR here (see "What did not work").

![funnel](results/figures/syn_02_screening_funnel.png)

### Hedge methods (same cohorts and dates, net)

| | Static OLS | Rolling OLS (60d, t−1) | Kalman (δ by formation likelihood) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Synthetic Sharpe / trades / turnover | 1.87 / 158 / 3.4× | 1.60 / 98 / 2.3× (187 re-hedges) | 1.00 / 7 / 0.2× |
| Market Sharpe / trades | −0.47 / 32 | −0.29 / 25 | −0.54 / 3 |
| Median nominal ADF p of trading-window spreads, syn / mkt | 0.19 / 0.62 | 0.07 / 0.28 | 0.0008 / 0.003 |

The Kalman spreads look "most stationary" but produced the fewest trades: the filter
absorbs divergence. ADF p-values on adaptive spreads are not valid tests.

![hedge paths](results/figures/syn_05_hedge_ratio_paths.png)
![Kalman example](results/figures/syn_05b_kalman_time_varying_beta.png)

### Cost sensitivity (flat one-way bps per leg **replacing** commission, spread and slippage; borrow and financing unchanged)

| bps | 0 | 5 | 10 | 20 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Synthetic Sharpe | 2.02 | 1.83 | 1.64 | 1.25 |
| Market Sharpe | −0.37 | −0.50 | −0.63 | −0.88 |

### Threshold grid (static OLS)

Tuned on VALIDATION cohorts only. Each choice was evaluated once out of sample. The full
outer-test grid is EXPLORATORY and was not used for the choice. 28 trials per data mode
are logged in `results/logs/trial_registry.jsonl`.

| | chosen on validation | validation Sharpe | OOS Sharpe of that choice | OOS Sharpe, a-priori (2.0/0) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Synthetic | entry 1.5, exit 0.25 | 1.98 | 2.11 | 1.87 |
| Market | entry 2.5, exit 0 | 0.96 | **−0.22** | −0.47 |

![validation heatmap](results/figures/syn_13_threshold_heatmap_validation.png)
![market exploratory heatmap](results/figures/mkt_13b_threshold_heatmap_EXPLORATORY_test.png)

### Stability of selected pairs

| | re-selected in next cohort (overlapping windows) | EG p<0.05 in next **non-overlapping** 12 months | median half-life, formation → forward |
|---|---|---|---|
| Synthetic | 25% | 34% | 5.4 → 9.1 |
| Market | 12% | 8% | 5.3 → 18.9 |

![stability](results/figures/mkt_17_pair_stability.png)

Other figures: `*_06_ou_fit_half_life`, `*_07_entries_exits_fills`, `*_09_drawdown`,
`*_11_cost_decomposition`, `*_12_cost_sensitivity`, `*_14_hedge_method_comparison`,
`*_16_rolling_beta_exposure`, `*_18_holding_and_trade_pnl`, `*_19_cohort_exposure_audit`,
`syn_01_cointegrated_vs_not`. Tables: `results/tables/` (see `docs/data_dictionary.md`),
including `syn_00_calibration.csv` and `20_what_did_not_work.csv`.

## What did not work (generated in `results/tables/20_what_did_not_work.csv`)

1. **No market edge.** OOS net excess Sharpe was −0.47 (static OLS), −0.29 (rolling) and
   −0.54 (Kalman). Gross was negative too, so this is not only a cost problem.
2. **Validation tuning did not transfer** on market data (0.96 → −0.22). The
   lookback-multiple grid was inert because every half-life put the window at its
   20-session floor.
3. **FDR was not controlled** on the synthetic universe (BH mean FDP 0.29 at q = 0.10).
   The EG test rejected 14–38% at nominal 5% when the null was "nearly cointegrated".
4. **Pairs did not stay cointegrated.** Only 8% (market) and 34% (synthetic) of selected
   pairs rejected no-cointegration in the following year. Half-lives lengthened.
5. **The Kalman filter did not beat OLS.** Innovation |z| ≥ 2 occurred on only 0.2–2.3%
   of pair-days.
6. **The 3.5σ stop never fired** for static OLS. Even with a 20-session window it is
   rarely reachable. A first version with a 10-session floor made it *impossible*
   (|z| ≤ 2.85). This was found and fixed after results had been seen, and is disclosed in
   `docs/bias_controls.md`.
7. **Capital was under-used** (average gross 12% synthetic, 3% market).
8. **24 of 42 market formation dates selected nothing.** The account stayed in cash,
   which is a valid outcome.

## Plain-English explanations

* **Correlation vs cointegration.** Correlated returns mean two prices move together day
  to day. Cointegration means the *gap* between the prices keeps getting pulled back.
  Example C has 0.79 return correlation and no cointegration.
* **Why residual tests need different critical values.** The regression picks the
  coefficients that make the residual as flat as possible, so even unrelated series look
  "stationary-ish". Stricter (more negative) cut-offs correct for that.
* **What FDR does and does not control.** Among the pairs you call discoveries *on one
  date*, BH keeps the *expected share* of false ones at or below q, if the p-values are
  valid. It does not cover repeated screens, it does not fix bad p-values, and it says
  nothing about profitability.
* **OU half-life.** The time it takes for a typical gap to shrink by half
  (ln 2 / speed of mean reversion).
* **Filtering vs smoothing.** A filter uses data up to today. A smoother also uses future
  data, which is fine for describing the past and cheating for trading.
* **Why log-price β is not a share ratio.** β links percentage moves. To hedge, short β
  dollars of B per dollar of A, which is β·P_A/P_B shares.
* **Dollar vs market neutrality.** Equal long and short dollars is not zero market beta
  when the two stocks have different betas. A β-weighted hedge is not dollar-neutral
  unless β = 1.
* **A stationary spread can still lose money.** Costs, gaps at the open, borrow, too-slow
  reversion (time stops), and breaks that happen exactly while you hold the position.
* **Why dynamic hedge ratios create turnover.** Every change in β means trading shares
  of the second leg, and each trade pays costs (187 re-hedges for rolling OLS).
* **Shorting and financing.** The short leg pays a borrow fee, and short proceeds may not
  earn interest. Long cash earns interest, so "net" returns must be read against cash.
* **No pairs or negative OOS results are valid findings.** Forcing trades or retuning
  until something works would make the reported numbers meaningless.

## Data provenance

* **Synthetic:** `pairs.data.generate_universe`, seed 20261122, labelled SYNTHETIC
  everywhere. The generating truth is saved next to the data.
* **Market:** Yahoo Finance via yfinance 1.8.0, retrieved 2026-11-22 (UTC timestamp in
  `data/market/market_us.meta.json`), 2012-01-03 to 2025-12-31. OHLC are split- and
  dividend-adjusted. USD only. Industry labels were assigned by the author from current
  classifications (not point-in-time). **Survivorship bias is present.** The cache is
  for local research; do not redistribute provider data.

## Bias safeguards (details in `docs/bias_controls.md`)

Formation-only screening (asserted and tested by mutating future data). A point-in-time
view inside the backtester. Next-open fills. A filter-only Kalman tuned on formation
data. Predeclared orientation with one p-value per pair. FDR per formation date. Trial
registry. Validation/OOS/exploratory labels. A locked, physically removed hold-out. One
shared account for overlapping cohorts. One post-hoc design revision, disclosed.

## Quality checks (this machine, Python 3.14.4, Windows 11)

* `pytest`: **61 passed**, 0 failed, 0 skipped, including 2 slow null-calibration tests.
* Coverage: 66% overall. Core modules 85–98%; the experiment orchestrator (15%) and plot
  helpers (48%) are exercised by the pipeline runs, not by unit tests.
* `ruff`: all checks passed.
* `mypy`: **149 errors remain**, mainly pandas-stubs strictness (`.at[]` scalar
  unions, `**kwargs` inference, `np.log(DataFrame)` typing). They are not resolved; none
  that I inspected was a runtime defect, but not all were inspected.
* The backtester's own test suite: 93 passed against the installed wheel.

